  {"type": "split", "photo": "sys", "h2": "When we'll tell a Hamilton homeowner to stop repairing the AC",
   "html": "<p>We fix a lot of air conditioners that other companies would have condemned. A capacitor, a contactor, a fan motor or a drain on a ten-year-old unit is routine, and we'd rather do it than sell you a system you don't need.</p>"
           "<p>The line gets crossed in three places. First, a compressor failure on a unit past 12 years. Second, a leak inside the evaporator coil on an R-22 system, where the refrigerant alone makes the repair poor value. Third, a unit that was oversized from day one and has never kept a lower-city home dry, because no repair fixes a sizing mistake.</p>"
           "<p>At that point we show two prices: a like-for-like central AC, and a cold-climate heat pump on the same ducts. For a radiator home with ductless heads, it's a new multi-zone system. The heat pump usually wins because it cools identically, cuts gas use in spring and fall, and can bring back a rebate through the Home Renovation Savings Program if the model is on NRCan's qualified list and pre-approval is done before installation.</p>"
           "<ul class=\"checklist\"><li>Small electrical part on a unit under 12 years: repair</li>"
           "<li>Compressor or coil on an older unit: compare both prices</li>"
           "<li>R-22 leak: usually replace</li>"
           "<li>Oversized and clammy: resize, don't patch</li></ul>"},
